    "https://cityu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203408": {
      "title": "Associate Faculty, School of Technology and Computing",
      "description": "City University of Seattle\nJob Posting: Associate Faculty, School of Technology and Computing City University of Seattle \n\tJob Posting: Associate Faculty, School of Technology and Computing Title Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Associate Faculty, School of Technology and Computing Category: Part-Time Teaching Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: School of Technology and Computing Locations: Seattle, WA Posted: Aug 5, 2026 Closes: Jun 30, 2027 - 11:59 PM EDT Type: Part-time Position ID: 203408 Share About City University of Seattle: City University of Seattle (CityU) is a distinguished and comprehensive private nonprofit university and an affiliate of National University. We serve students regionally, nationally, and globally, reaching across borders to impart knowledge and empower individuals. With a focus on adult learners and online students, we provide flexible, accessible programs tailored to meet the needs of the modern learner. Offering over 60 academic degree programs, we are committed to expanding our student's knowledge, skills, and possibilities. Our mission is deeply rooted in the belief that education can improve lives. We invite you to join us in our commitment to fostering community, promoting diversity, and providing an environment where every student can thrive and achieve their educational goals. Job Description: About the School of Technology & Computing The School of Technology & Computing (STC) brings together technology education and real-world industry experience. Our programs are designed for working professionals and career changers who want to build practical, relevant skills and apply them directly to today's technology landscape. STC offers undergraduate and graduate programs in Artificial Intelligence, Computer Science, Data Science, and Cybersecurity , with flexible online and in-person learning options. What sets STC apart is our strong connection to the technology industry. Our students learn from faculty who bring current professional experience into the classroom, connecting academic concepts to the tools, challenges, and practices they encounter in the real world. We also welcome students entering technology from nontraditional backgrounds, with bridge and preparatory courses that help them build the foundation they need to succeed. For technology professionals and educators, STC offers an opportunity to teach students who are serious about advancing their careers. Faculty in the Greater Seattle area may have opportunities to teach online or in person, while faculty with doctoral-level expertise may also contribute to our Doctor of Information Technology program. STC is part of a university built around the needs of adult learner students who bring professional experience, diverse perspectives, and clear goals into the classroom. Our faculty play an important role in helping these students turn their experience and ambition into meaningful career opportunities. Job Summary Associate Faculty in STC bring their professional expertise and teaching experience into the classroom to create an engaging, practical learning experience for adult students preparing to advance their careers in technology and computing. Faculty are expected to connect academic concepts with real-world practice, use effective and engaging teaching approaches, and support students through mentoring and meaningful interaction. Teaching opportunities may be available in online, mixed-mode, and on-campus formats. Success in this role requires an understanding of adult learners, cultural awareness, and the ability to bridge technology skills with the broader knowledge and concepts that help students succeed in the field. Associate Faculty play an important role in helping STC students build the knowledge, confidence, and practical skills they need for meaningful careers in technology and computing. Requirements: Minimum Qualifications Master's degree from an accredited institution in computing or a related field, such as Artificial Intelligence, Data Science, Computer Science, Cybersecurity, Information Technology, Software Engineering, or Computer Engineering. A bachelor's degree in a STEM or related technical field is preferred. Relevant professional experience in one or more areas of technology and computing, with the ability to bring current industry knowledge and practices into the classroom. Ability to connect academic concepts with practical, real-world applications and engage adult learners through effective teaching practices. A genuine understanding of the practitioner-faculty model and the importance of providing students with the highest quality educational experience in technology and computing. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate effectively with students, faculty, staff, and university leadership. Ability to collaborate effectively with colleagues and engage respectfully with students from diverse cultural, professional, and educational backgrounds. Desired Qualifications Experience as both an online student and an online instructor. Experience using learning management systems and other educational technologies. Doctoral degree in related technical fields such as Artificial Intelligence, Computer Science, Data Science, Cybersecurity, and Information Technology. Teaching or training experience in higher education, professional education, or industry. Experience teaching in both online and in-person environments. Minimum of two years of professional experience in the technology industry. Relevant industry-recognized certifications. Additional Information: Physical Demands: The physical demands described here represent those that an employee must meet to perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is frequently required to stand, sit, talk, hear, and perform repetitive motions of the hands or wrists. The employee is occasionally required to stand, walk, grasp or handle, stoop or bend, and reach. Working Conditions: The working conditions described here represent those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Work is performed indoors in a remote and/or online classroom setting. Salary Information: Associate Faculty compensation is based on course level (undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral), the number of course credits, delivery mode, and the number of students in class. Course rates range from  $2,300 to $4,300. Application Instructions: City University of Seattle accepts applications through this online process only. For consideration, please submit a complete application packet. A complete application includes the following: Completed Electronic Application Form Resume Cover Letter Once you have created a profile, please remember to record your password. Responses will be sent to your Interview Exchange account if you use the 'Help Desk' feature. A hiring manager will contact candidates with qualifications and experience most aligned with current needs. Once the position is filled, you will see a status update in your account profile. If you encounter technical problems while applying for a position, please use the 'Help Desk' feature in your Interview Exchange account to submit the issue to technical support. For non-technical issues or questions, please email employment@cityu.edu. City University of Seattle is an equal opportunity employer and is continuously working to build a diverse faculty and staff. City University strongly encourages inquiries from all qualified individuals interested in becoming part of the CityU team, including women, Aboriginal peoples, persons with disabilities, and members of visible minorities. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation City University of Seattle 521 Wall St., Suite 100 Seattle, WA 98121"
    },
    "https://cityu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203346": {
      "title": "Associate Faculty, DBA",
      "description": "City University of Seattle\nJob Posting: Associate Faculty, DBA City University of Seattle \n\tJob Posting: Associate Faculty, DBA Title Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Associate Faculty, DBA Category: Part-Time Teaching Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: School of Business and Management Locations: Seattle, WA Posted: Aug 4, 2026 Closes: Jun 30, 2027 - 11:59 PM EDT Type: Part-time Position ID: 203346 Share About City University of Seattle: City University of Seattle (CityU) is a distinguished and comprehensive private nonprofit university and an affiliate of the National University. We serve students regionally, nationally, and globally, reaching across borders to impart knowledge and empower individuals. With a focus on adult learners and online students, we provide flexible, accessible programs tailored to meet the needs of the modern learner. Offering over 60 academic degree programs, we are committed to expanding our student's knowledge, skills, and possibilities. Our mission is deeply rooted in the belief that education can improve lives. We invite you to join us in our commitment to fostering community, promoting diversity, and providing an environment where every student can thrive and achieve their educational goals. Job Description: You've built a career solving real-world business problems. Now you're ready to share your expertise and shape the next generation of business leaders. City University of Seattle's School of Business and Management is seeking experienced business leaders to join our Doctor of Business Administration (DBA) program as Associate Faculty. This opportunity is ideal for professionals with a doctoral degree who have built their expertise through industry leadership, consulting, entrepreneurship, or other applied professional experience. Why Teach with Us Mentor motivated, career-driven professionals. Work with accomplished adult learners who bring years of workplace experience to the classroom and are pursuing their doctorate to advance their careers, deepen their expertise, and lead at the highest levels. Turn your experience into impact. Share real-world insights, case studies, and lessons learned that students could apply immediately in their organizations, bridging the gap between scholarly theory and executive practice. Teach on a schedule that fits your life. Our fully online DBA program, with a flexible format, lets you contribute meaningfully to a doctoral program while continuing your professional career from anywhere. Join a collaborative, inclusive academic community. Become part of a supportive network of scholar-practitioners and faculty colleagues who value diverse perspectives, shared learning, and a genuine commitment to student success. Shape the next generation of business leaders. Guide students toward their personal, academic, and professional goals, and play a direct role in developing the leaders who will drive the future of business and industry. What You'll Do Facilitate engaging, practice-based lectures that connect scholarly theory to real-world business applications. Mentor and advise doctoral candidates as they develop their scholarly voice and research capabilities. Guide students in the design, conduct, and communication of applied business research. Provide timely, constructive feedback that advances both academic rigor and professional growth. Foster a collaborative, inclusive online learning environment that supports student success. Contribute to the continuous improvement of curriculum, assessment, and student experience. Teaching and Mentorship Areas May Include Faculty expertise is welcomed across a range of doctoral-level areas, including: Business management, leadership, and strategy Organizational development and change management Applied and doctoral-level business research Data-driven and evidence-based decision-making Ethics and responsible leadership Global business and emerging challenges Technology and organizational transformation Innovation, project-based initiatives, and thought leadership Dissertation research and development Requirements: Minimum Qualifications To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below represent the knowledge, skills, and abilities required. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Earned doctorate (PhD, DBA, or equivalent) in business, management, or a closely related discipline from a regionally accredited institution or international equivalent. Minimum of 10 years of relevant professional experience; 20 or more years preferred. Understanding of the practitioner-faculty model, with a genuine passion for teaching, and a commitment to maintaining current knowledge in one's field. Excellent verbal and written communication skills, including a working knowledge of APA 7th edition style. Strong presentation and interpersonal skills, with the ability to relate to and communicate effectively with diverse students, faculty, and staff. Proficiency with Microsoft Office products and learning management systems. Desired Qualifications Experience guiding students through applied research dissertations. An established record of publication and presentation, supported by a currently active research agenda. Substantive executive, consulting, or senior leadership experience that enriches applied research mentorship. Additional Information: Physical Demands: The physical demands described here represent those that an employee must meet to perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is frequently required to stand, sit, talk, hear, and perform repetitive motions of the hands or wrists. The employee is occasionally required to stand, walk, grasp or handle, stoop or bend, and reach. Working Conditions: The working conditions described here represent those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Work is generally performed remotely. Weekend hours may be required depending on class availability. Salary Information: Part-time Associate Faculty salary is based on the number of course credits (1-6) and the number of students in the class. The pay range is $600-$6,000 per course. Application Instructions: City University of Seattle accepts applications through this online process only. For consideration, please submit a complete application packet. A complete application includes the following: Completed Electronic Application Form Resume Cover Letter Once you have created a profile, please remember to record your password. Responses will be sent to your Interview Exchange account if you use the 'Help Desk' feature. A hiring manager will contact candidates with qualifications and experience most aligned with current needs. Once the position is filled, you will see a status update in your account profile. If you encounter technical problems while applying for a position, please use the 'Help Desk' feature in your Interview Exchange account to submit the issue to technical support. For non-technical issues or questions, please email employment@cityu.edu. City University of Seattle is an equal opportunity employer and is continuously working to build a diverse faculty and staff. City University strongly encourages inquiries from all qualified individuals interested in becoming part of the CityU team, including women, Aboriginal peoples, persons with disabilities, and members of visible minorities. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation City University of Seattle 521 Wall St., Suite 100 Seattle, WA 98121"
    },
    "https://cityu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203345": {
      "title": "Dissertation Faculty, DBA",
      "description": "City University of Seattle\nJob Posting: Dissertation Faculty, DBA City University of Seattle \n\tJob Posting: Dissertation Faculty, DBA Title Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Dissertation Faculty, DBA Category: Part-Time Teaching Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: School of Business and Management Locations: Seattle, WA Posted: Aug 4, 2026 Closes: Jun 30, 2027 - 11:59 PM EDT Type: Part-time Position ID: 203345 Share About City University of Seattle: City University of Seattle (CityU) is a distinguished and comprehensive private nonprofit university and an affiliate of the National University. We serve students regionally, nationally, and globally, reaching across borders to impart knowledge and empower individuals. With a focus on adult learners and online students, we provide flexible, accessible programs tailored to meet the needs of the modern learner. Offering over 60 academic degree programs, we are committed to expanding our student's knowledge, skills, and possibilities. Our mission is deeply rooted in the belief that education can improve lives. We invite you to join us in our commitment to fostering community, promoting diversity, and providing an environment where every student can thrive and achieve their educational goals. Job Description: Help shape the next generation of business leaders and scholars! City University of Seattle's School of Business and Management is seeking Dissertation Chairs and Committee Members to mentor DBA students through impactful, applied research and successful dissertation completion. The Dissertation Chair works with each student and their Doctoral Committee Members to ensure that the student designs and executes a defensible, applied research dissertation, following accepted research practices, CityU policies and formats, and Institutional Review Board (IRB) requirements. What You'll Do: Dissertation Chair The Dissertation Chair serves as the primary mentor and academic guide for the doctoral candidate, leading the committee and shepherding the student from topic development through a successful defense. Responsibilities include: Coach and advise the doctoral candidate across all components of the dissertation, from topic discovery through completion, offering expertise in research methods, theoretical frameworks, and analysis relevant to the student's topic. Guide the student in meeting deadlines, academic milestones, and program requirements. Recommend resources and support services to strengthen the candidate's writing, research, and analytical skills. Coordinate and communicate with Committee Members, the Program Director, the IRB, and other university stakeholders. Ensure efficient review of student work from proposal through dissertation defense by providing constructive feedback within established turnaround expectations and offering guidance on presentation and defense techniques. Lead the committee in a rigorous, fair, and objective evaluation of the student's work. Uphold the highest standards of academic integrity, ethics, and professional conduct. Dissertation Committee Members Committee members work in partnership with the Dissertation Chair to guide and evaluate the student throughout the dissertation process. Responsibilities include: Serve as a member of the Dissertation Committee and support the student and Chair throughout the dissertation journey. Provide subject matter expertise relevant to the student's research topic, theoretical framework, and methodology. Review milestone documents and provide timely, constructive feedback to the student and Chair within established turnaround expectations. Participate in committee meetings and consultations as needed. Assess and participate in the student's proposal and dissertation defense. Contribute to a rigorous, fair, and objective evaluation process. Uphold the highest standards of academic integrity, ethics, and professional conduct. Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below represent the knowledge, skills, and abilities required. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Earned doctorate (PhD, DBA, EdD, or equivalent) in business, management, or a closely related discipline from a regionally accredited institution or international equivalent. Demonstrated methodological competence in at least one research tradition (qualitative, quantitative, or mixed methods) sufficient to guide design, data collection, and analysis. Working knowledge of IRB processes and human subjects research protections, with a willingness to complete CITI (or equivalent) training prior to first assignment. Commitment to maintaining current knowledge in one's field through active engagement with professional organizations and scholarly or practitioner communities. Excellent oral and written communication skills, including proficiency in APA 7th edition style and scholarly writing conventions. A student-centered approach and the ability to communicate effectively with diverse students, faculty, and staff. Availability to commit to a multi-term mentoring relationship through defense, with demonstrated ability to work effectively in an online, largely asynchronous environment. Understanding the practitioner-faculty model and a dedication to fostering a supportive, inclusive learning environment. Desired Qualifications: Experience chairing doctoral dissertations to successful completion, particularly with applied or practitioner-oriented candidates. An established record of peer-reviewed publication, conference presentation, or manuscript and proposal review, supported by an active research agenda. Familiarity with alternative dissertation models and formats, such as the Three-Article Dissertation (TAD). Substantive executive, consulting, or senior practitioner experience in business or industry that supports applied research mentorship. Experience teaching or advising adult and working professional learners. Familiarity with Brightspace (D2L) or a comparable LMS. Experience with accreditation-aligned assessment or programmatic review. Additional Information: Physical Demands: The physical demands described here represent those that an employee must meet to perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is frequently required to stand, sit, talk, hear, and perform repetitive motions of the hands or wrists. The employee is occasionally required to stand, walk, grasp or handle, stoop or bend, and reach. Working Conditions: The working conditions described here are representative of those an employee encounter while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Work is generally performed remotely. Weekend hours may be required. A personal computer, reliable internet connection, video capabilities, and a working phone. Compensation: Committee Chairs are given a stipend of $4,200 per student. Committee Members are given a stipend of $800 per student. Payments occur based on milestones. Application Instructions: City University of Seattle accepts applications through this online process only. For consideration, please submit a complete application packet. A complete application includes: Completed Electronic Application Form Resume Cover Letter Once you have created a profile, please remember to record your password. Responses will be sent to your Interview Exchange account if you use the 'Help Desk' feature. A hiring manager will contact candidates with qualifications and experience most aligned with current needs. Once the position is filled, you will see a status update in your account profile. If you encounter technical problems while applying for a position, please use the 'Help Desk' feature in your Interview Exchange account to submit the issue to technical support. For non-technical issues or questions, please email employment@cityu.edu. City University of Seattle is an equal opportunity employer and is continuously working to build a diverse faculty and staff. City University strongly encourages inquiries from all qualified individuals interested in becoming part of the CityU team, including women, Aboriginal peoples, persons with disabilities, and members of visible minorities. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation City University of Seattle 521 Wall St., Suite 100 Seattle, WA 98121"
    },
    "https://cityu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205524": {
      "title": "Associate Faculty, MEd in Leadership, School Climate and Culture",
      "description": "City University of Seattle\nJob Posting: Associate Faculty, MEd in Leadership, School Climate and Culture City University of Seattle \n\tJob Posting: Associate Faculty, MEd in Leadership, School Climate and Culture Title Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Associate Faculty, MEd in Leadership, School Climate and Culture Category: Part-Time Teaching Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: School of Education and Leadership Locations: Seattle, WA Posted: Oct 5, 2026 Closes: Dec 31, 2026 - 11:59 PM EST Type: Part-time Position ID: 205524 Share About City University of Seattle: City University of Seattle (CityU) is a distinguished and comprehensive private nonprofit university and an affiliate of the National University. We serve students regionally, nationally, and globally, reaching across borders to impart knowledge and empower individuals. With a focus on adult learners and online students, we provide flexible, accessible programs tailored to meet the needs of the modern learner. Offering over 60 academic degree programs, we are committed to expanding our student's knowledge, skills, and possibilities. Our mission is deeply rooted in the belief that education can improve lives. We invite you to join us in our commitment to fostering community, promoting diversity, and providing an environment where every student can thrive and achieve their educational goals. Job Description: City University of Seattle's School of Education and Leadership is seeking an experienced educational leader to serve as a part-time Associate Faculty member in our Master of Education (MEd) in Leadership program for course EEA 519 - School Climate and Culture. A positive school climate and culture can shape how students learn, how educators work together, and how families and communities experience their schools. This course explores the practices and systems educational leaders can use to build positive, inclusive, and supportive learning communities where all students can thrive. As an Associate Faculty member, you will help graduate students examine the relationship between school climate, culture, student achievement, relationships, and well-being. Students explore topics including social-emotional learning, cultural diversity, equity, restorative justice, and systems of support, while learning how to use research and data to identify opportunities for meaningful school improvement. You will guide students in connecting these concepts to their own educational settings and developing practical strategies?including professional development?to strengthen school climate and culture. In this course, students will: Examine research on the impact of social-emotional learning on student achievement and behavior Explore the relationship between school climate, culture, relationships, achievement, and student well-being Consider the perspectives of students, educators, families, and communities in understanding school climate and culture Apply tools and data to assess school climate and culture and identify opportunities for school improvement Explore culturally responsive, equitable, and restorative approaches to building supportive learning communities Design professional development to support improvements in school climate and culture What We're Looking For We are looking for an experienced educational leader who understands that a strong school community is built through relationships, inclusive practices, thoughtful leadership, and intentional systems of support. Requirements: Required qualifications: Master's degree in Educational Leadership, Education, or a closely related field Experience in school-level and/or district-level educational leadership Preferred qualifications: Experience teaching, mentoring, coaching, or developing educators Experience leading or supporting initiatives related to school climate, culture, social-emotional learning, student well-being, equity, restorative practices, or community engagement Experience using school climate or student data to inform school improvement Experience designing or facilitating professional development for educators Experience teaching or facilitating learning in an online or hybrid environment Additional Information: Physical Demands: The physical demands described here represent those that an employee must meet to perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is frequently required to stand, sit, talk, hear, and perform repetitive motions of the hands or wrists. The employee is occasionally required to stand, walk, grasp or handle, stoop or bend, and reach. Working Conditions: The working conditions described here represent those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Work is generally performed indoors in a typical office and classroom setting. Weekend hours may be required depending on class availability. Salary Information: Associate Faculty compensation ranges from $500 to $4,600 per course assigned. Compensation varies based on factors including course level (undergraduate or graduate), number of course credits, and enrollment. Application Instructions: City University of Seattle accepts applications through this online process only. For consideration, please submit a complete application packet. A complete application includes the following: Completed Electronic Application Form Resume Cover Letter Once you have created a profile, please remember to record your password. Responses will be sent to your Interview Exchange account if you use the 'Help Desk' feature. A hiring manager will contact candidates with qualifications and experience most aligned with current needs. Once the position is filled, you will see a status update in your account profile. If you encounter technical problems while applying for a position, please use the 'Help Desk' feature in your Interview Exchange account to submit the issue to technical support. For non-technical issues or questions, please email employment@cityu.edu. City University of Seattle is an equal opportunity employer and is continuously working to build a diverse faculty and staff. City University strongly encourages inquiries from all qualified individuals interested in becoming part of the CityU team, including women, Aboriginal peoples, persons with disabilities, and members of visible minorities. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation City University of Seattle 521 Wall St., Suite 100 Seattle, WA 98121"
